## Definition
In VHDL, an architecture model describes the internal functionality of an entity, which is the basic building block of a digital circuit. 

## Explanation
The architecture model in VHDL defines how the inputs to an entity are transformed into outputs. It is used in conjunction with an entity declaration, which defines the interface of the circuit.

## Key Points
* There are several architecture models in VHDL, but primarily:
  • **Structural Architecture**: Describes the circuit in terms of its components and their interconnections.
  • **Behavioral Architecture**: Describes the circuit in terms of its behavior, using processes, procedures, and functions.
  • **Dataflow Architecture**: Describes the circuit in terms of the flow of data.

## Brief Example
For a simple circuit like a traffic light controller, a behavioral architecture using a state machine can be effectively used.

## VHDL Code for Traffic Light Controller

```vhdl
-- Entity Declaration
entity traffic_light is
    Port ( clk : in STD_LOGIC;
           reset : in STD_LOGIC;
           red : out STD_LOGIC;
           yellow : out STD_LOGIC;
           green : out STD_LOGIC);
end traffic_light;

-- Architecture Declaration
architecture Behavioral of traffic_light is
    type state_type is (red_state, yellow_state, green_state);
    signal state, next_state : state_type;
begin
    process(clk, reset)
    begin
        if reset = '1' then
            state <= red_state;
        elsif rising_edge(clk) then
            state <= next_state;
        end if;
    end process;

    process(state)
    begin
        case state is
            when red_state =>
                red <= '1';
                yellow <= '0';
                green <= '0';
                next_state <= green_state;
            when green_state =>
                red <= '0';
                yellow <= '0';
                green <= '1';
                next_state <= yellow_state;
            when yellow_state =>
                red <= '0';
                yellow <= '1';
                green <= '0';
                next_state <= red_state;
        end case;
    end process;
end Behavioral;
```

## Assumptions
- The traffic light controller has three outputs: red, yellow, and green lights.
- The controller uses a clock signal (clk) and a reset signal.
- The state machine has three states: red_state, green_state, and yellow_state.
- The controller starts in the red_state upon reset. 

This VHDL code uses a state machine to design a simple traffic light controller with a behavioral architecture model.

